cff-version: 1.2.0
message: If you use this code, cite Geogram.
title: Geogram subset for curvenet.elf (vendored)
abstract: >-
  Geogram 1.9.9 (upstream BrunoLevy/geogram c40b4653) as the org's Godot fork
  carries it under thirdparty/geogram, with that fork's -fno-exceptions
  patches. Subset: the 61 sources modules/cassie/SCsub compiles
  (basic minus geofile/android_utils/boolean_expression, numerics,
  mesh/mesh and mesh_reorder, delaunay minus tetgen/triangle/
  parallel_delaunay_3d, points minus co3ne, bibliography, OpenNL .c),
  plus delaunay/delaunay_2d.cpp, which SCsub left out; the 122 headers
  those sources and mwt's DelaunayFaces.cpp include, transitively; and
  zlib.h/zconf.h from thirdparty/zlib (headers only, geofile.h wants them,
  nothing links zlib). 185 files, 3,614,343 bytes before this file.
  tools/vendor/thirdparty_subset.sh reproduces the tree from the pinned
  commit. Local adaptation (tools/vendor/patches/geogram.patch):
  delaunay/delaunay.cpp registers the "BDEL2d" and "BPOW2d" creators again
  (the fork dropped them with delaunay_2d.cpp), so Cassie's
  delaunay_triangulate_2d_raw can create a Delaunay2d. Determinism (Gate 4
  compares the guest with a native build bit for bit): basic/algorithm.h
  random_shuffle (the BRIO insertion order) seeds a fixed mt19937 instead
  of std::random_device and shuffles by Fisher-Yates on its raw output
  instead of std::shuffle, and basic/numeric.cpp random_int32/float32/
  float64 map mt19937_64's raw output instead of std::uniform_*_distribution
  -- a cospherical boundary (a closed stroke on a sphere) triangulated
  differently on every call, and libc++ and libstdc++ draw different
  numbers from the same engine. basic/memory.h is upstream's again:
  aligned_malloc reaches posix_memalign in the guest, whose godot-sandbox
  fallback used to return an already-freed block (Gate 4: "Possible
  double-free" in ~Delaunay3d); that is fixed where it lives, in
  vendor/sandbox-api (see its CITATION.cff), so the carve-from-malloc
  workaround this subset carried is gone. mesh/mesh_reorder.cpp
  Hilbert_vcmp (the BRIO/Hilbert insertion order of every Delaunay here)
  breaks ties on a coordinate by the vertex index, a strict total order:
  reorder_split's std::nth_element leaves tied elements in an unspecified
  order, and libc++ and libstdc++ leave them differently, so points sharing
  a coordinate (a skirt panel's boundary: ring samples at one height, seam
  samples in one plane) were inserted in another order in the guest than
  natively, MingCurve got Geogram's Delaunay faces in another order (1263
  both ways), and DMWT tiled one panel differently (Gate 4 skirt_tube:
  1304 against 1307 welded vertices). Untied inputs sort as before; the
  other nine Gate 4 checks read the same numbers.
type: software
